01:02:28.850 --> 01:02:29.150
Yeah.

1581
01:02:29.410 --> 01:02:30.010
In November.

1582
01:02:30.290 --> 01:02:32.590
We got a lot of fan questions about this.

1583
01:02:33.210 --> 01:02:39.830
Charlia G., Catherine Otto, Kelly Hendricks, Andrea V., and Julie Brown all asked,

1584
01:02:40.030 --> 01:02:43.470
did Creed really dye his hair for this episode?

1585
01:02:44.870 --> 01:02:46.550
Well, we texted Creed.

1586
01:02:46.790 --> 01:02:47.130
We did.

1587
01:02:47.270 --> 01:02:49.870
We had a delightful text thread with him yesterday.

1588
01:02:50.390 --> 01:02:51.810
It was so funny.

1589
01:02:51.890 --> 01:02:52.290
I know.

1590
01:02:52.690 --> 01:02:57.970
Well, he said that it was a vegetable dye that washed out completely no problem,

1591
01:02:57.970 --> 01:03:06.170
but he suggested that we tell you all that it was a permanent dye that caused all of his hair to fall out

1592
01:03:06.170 --> 01:03:14.530
so that he could possibly use this as evidence to sue Tim Ferry, who would then countersue him for libel.

1593
01:03:14.530 --> 01:03:19.310
And I was like, you're turning our podcast into a courtroom thriller.

1594
01:03:19.610 --> 01:03:31.070
I think Creed yesterday was really tickled because he had a whole text thread with us where he went on and on about his different ideas of like a better story of how they dyed his hair.

1595
01:03:31.570 --> 01:03:32.010
Yes.

1596
01:03:32.190 --> 01:03:33.990
He was having a good time texting us.

1597
01:03:34.070 --> 01:03:37.350
But the truth was, it was just a washout vegetable dye.

1598
01:03:38.430 --> 01:03:39.190
Sorry, Creed.

1599
01:03:39.410 --> 01:03:45.090
And of course, we find out in this conference room scene that Creed used all the ink from the printer.

1600
01:03:46.010 --> 01:03:46.450
Yeah.

1601
01:03:46.610 --> 01:03:47.690
He put the printer ink.

1602
01:03:47.890 --> 01:03:53.770
There is a deleted scene, Jenna, where he has a mirror and he's filling in the spots he missed with a black sharpie.

1603
01:03:53.770 --> 01:03:55.030
Oh, my gosh.

1604
01:03:55.090 --> 01:03:55.530
I know.

1605
01:03:55.710 --> 01:03:56.150
I know.

1606
01:03:57.230 --> 01:03:58.110
Oh, my goodness.

1607
01:03:58.230 --> 01:04:03.530
Well, we might have to start a different text thread with him about that black sharpie deleted scene because I need to know more.

1608
01:04:03.790 --> 01:04:07.650
By the way, we not only had a text thread with Creed yesterday, just the three of us,

1609
01:04:07.710 --> 01:04:11.470
we also had a lot of activity on our group office text thread.

1610
01:04:11.550 --> 01:04:14.490
We were like all texting each other up a storm.

1611
01:04:14.810 --> 01:04:18.530
And I had a separate text thread with just the accountants going.

1612
01:04:18.850 --> 01:04:20.430
Oh, my gosh.

1613
01:04:22.050 --> 01:04:24.590
We were text happy as a cast yesterday.

1614
01:04:24.850 --> 01:04:25.810
I guess we were.

1615
01:04:26.150 --> 01:04:29.550
Well, like Angela said, everyone is gathered in the conference room.

1616
01:04:30.130 --> 01:04:32.350
Ryan enters to do his BlackBerry tutorial.

1617
01:04:32.450 --> 01:04:36.870
But Michael tells Ryan, I'm sorry, this is still my office.

1618
01:04:37.150 --> 01:04:37.770
Have a seat.

1619
01:04:38.270 --> 01:04:41.210
I could not believe that Ryan entertained this.

1620
01:04:41.550 --> 01:04:42.890
I couldn't believe it.

1621
01:04:43.290 --> 01:04:43.550
Yeah.

1622
01:04:43.690 --> 01:04:46.450
I mean, as soon as he saw the pictures on the wall.

1623
01:04:46.730 --> 01:04:47.050
Yeah.

1624
01:04:47.210 --> 01:04:49.790
He was like, oh, Michael, here we go.

1625
01:04:49.790 --> 01:04:50.450
Here we go.

1626
01:04:50.590 --> 01:04:51.990
It's the lady from Titanic.

1627
01:04:52.630 --> 01:04:53.210
Mm-hmm.

1628
01:04:54.110 --> 01:04:55.470
The Where's the Beef lady.

1629
01:04:55.710 --> 01:04:55.910
Yeah.

1630
01:04:56.090 --> 01:04:59.250
And then the picture from Big where they're dancing on the keyboard.

1631
01:04:59.870 --> 01:05:00.310
Yes.

1632
01:05:00.490 --> 01:05:02.130
And then the last one is Ben Kingsley.

1633
01:05:03.010 --> 01:05:03.450
Yeah.

1634
01:05:04.370 --> 01:05:08.430
And this is when Pam is like, why did you reuse the old photos?

1635
01:05:08.510 --> 01:05:11.390
And Michael says, because the ink ran out of the printer.

1636
01:05:11.750 --> 01:05:11.910
Yeah.

1637
01:05:12.010 --> 01:05:13.950
And Creed kind of is like, there's like.

1638
01:05:15.630 --> 01:05:20.370
But then all of a sudden, someone enters the room, just as Ryan has had enough.

1639
01:05:20.790 --> 01:05:26.610
In walks Robert Dunder, one of the founders of Dunder Mifflin.

1640
01:05:26.970 --> 01:05:29.470
How fantastic was that scene with him?

1641
01:05:29.610 --> 01:05:30.770
It was so good.

1642
01:05:30.770 --> 01:05:31.590
Oh, man.

1643
01:05:31.770 --> 01:05:36.230
I rewatched it like a few times because he's so perfect.

1644
01:05:36.230 --> 01:05:39.210
His delivery is spot on.

1645
01:05:39.770 --> 01:05:43.850
Well, Robert Dunder was played by this actor named John Engel.

1646
01:05:44.070 --> 01:05:47.530
And this is the only episode that Robert Dunder appears in.

1647
01:05:47.530 --> 01:05:51.670
But John Engel starred on General Hospital.

1648
01:05:52.100 --> 01:05:56.090
He played patriarch Edward Quartermain.

1649
01:05:56.610 --> 01:06:04.510
From 1993 to 2012, he did 485 episodes of General Hospital.

1650
01:06:04.690 --> 01:06:05.230
Wow.

1651
01:06:05.470 --> 01:06:09.970
He also did a ton of other television work.

1652
01:06:09.970 --> 01:06:19.790
He was also an acting teacher at Beverly Hills High School from 1964 to 1985 and listened to who he taught while he was a teacher there.

1653
01:06:20.010 --> 01:06:30.670
He taught Nicholas Cage, Albert Brooks, Swoozie Kurtz, David Schwimmer, and Jonathan Silverman, among others.

1654
01:06:30.910 --> 01:06:34.190
He was also an active volunteer for Habitat for Humanity.

1655
01:06:34.370 --> 01:06:38.930
He had five daughters and he passed away in 2012.

1656
01:06:39.970 --> 01:06:42.010
Well, we were so fortunate to have him.

1657
01:06:42.290 --> 01:06:45.850
He was such a seasoned actor and so, so delightful.

1658
01:06:46.390 --> 01:06:48.430
And I love the scene.

1659
01:06:49.330 --> 01:06:54.470
He goes on this very, very long, meandering story.

1660
01:06:55.170 --> 01:06:59.390
But the way he connects it all is so real and organic.

1661
01:06:59.650 --> 01:07:01.610
And you guys, we have been there.

1662
01:07:01.770 --> 01:07:07.910
We have all been there at that, you know, family gathering where someone gets up to talk and you're like,

1663
01:07:07.910 --> 01:07:10.550
what, what are we, what are we talking about?

1664
01:07:11.270 --> 01:07:15.830
Yeah, you ask some innocent question like, oh my gosh, where'd you get your sweater?

1665
01:07:16.110 --> 01:07:21.850
And they're like, well, it was three years ago and it was raining that day.

1666
01:07:21.850 --> 01:07:22.570
I remember.

1667
01:07:22.810 --> 01:07:27.670
I remember because I had just gotten my hair done and they gave me a plastic net to put over it.

1668
01:07:28.630 --> 01:07:38.430
And my friend Cynthia called and she said, well, you know, Cynthia, she had lived abroad and when she came back, she divorced and married that younger guy.

1669
01:07:39.550 --> 01:07:40.890
Well, she did.

1670
01:07:40.890 --> 01:07:47.870
But first, remember, she had that period of time where she went on that sex sabbatical.

1671
01:07:48.030 --> 01:07:48.610
She did.

1672
01:07:48.790 --> 01:07:50.750
That's right about when she got that mole removed.

1673
01:07:50.930 --> 01:07:52.530
Do you remember her dermatologist?

1674
01:07:52.530 --> 01:07:54.470
He was such a nice guy.

1675
01:07:55.210 --> 01:07:56.250
Tall guy.

1676
01:07:56.510 --> 01:07:57.290
Tall guy.

1677
01:07:57.630 --> 01:07:57.870
Tall.

1678
01:07:58.650 --> 01:08:00.090
And had an accent.

1679
01:08:00.950 --> 01:08:01.510
Yeah.

1680
01:08:01.790 --> 01:08:03.770
But I could never figure out where he was from.

1681
01:08:04.550 --> 01:08:05.110
Yeah.

1682
01:08:05.410 --> 01:08:06.910
He made a very good dip.

1683
01:08:07.690 --> 01:08:08.950
And you're like, what?

1684
01:08:09.250 --> 01:08:11.370
You're like, where's the sweater from?

1685
01:08:11.570 --> 01:08:12.450
Where's the sweater?

1686
01:08:12.690 --> 01:08:13.390
What did we just do?

1687
01:08:13.510 --> 01:08:18.290
Jenna and I just completely improvised this random conversation.

1688
01:08:18.290 --> 01:08:20.790
But you guys know you've been in this moment.

1689
01:08:20.790 --> 01:08:24.190
Yeah, that was Robert Dunder.

1690
01:08:24.370 --> 01:08:26.170
And so Michael throws him out.

1691
01:08:26.390 --> 01:08:29.010
Wait, can we also, sorry, this is hopping around.

1692
01:08:29.210 --> 01:08:32.690
Can we also talk about how Michael introduces Robert to everyone?

1693
01:08:33.230 --> 01:08:33.770
Please.

1694
01:08:34.070 --> 01:08:37.330
He says, who is this worthless bag of bones?

1695
01:08:38.690 --> 01:08:43.250
That's his introduction to the founder of the company.

1696
01:08:43.250 --> 01:08:50.850
Well, we also had a fan question query from Erin Kearney, who says,

1697
01:08:51.330 --> 01:08:59.430
Robert Dunder mentions Robert Mifflin's wife and her husband, Jerry Truppiano, a South Jersey guy who was tall.

1698
01:08:59.430 --> 01:09:07.630
My question is, was this a reference to Jerry Truppiano, the longtime Red Sox broadcaster?

1699
01:09:07.870 --> 01:09:14.750
I thought it might be because so many of the cast members and writers were Boston area folks.

1700
01:09:14.750 --> 01:09:21.170
Well, I believe it to be true because Mike Schur, who wrote this episode, is a huge baseball fan.

1701
01:09:21.650 --> 01:09:22.170
Yeah.

1702
01:09:22.350 --> 01:09:25.010
And I 100% think that's true.

1703
01:09:25.290 --> 01:09:26.850
I think that's a good catch.

1704
01:09:27.310 --> 01:09:27.550
Yeah.

1705
01:09:29.510 --> 01:09:30.330
Good catch.

1706
01:09:30.530 --> 01:09:32.910
Yeah, he snuck in a baseball player in there.

1707
01:09:34.470 --> 01:09:40.350
Well, as everyone is leaving the conference room, Ryan tells Michael, this day was a wash.

1708
01:09:40.730 --> 01:09:44.570
Michael needs to embrace the new technology as the way of the future.

1709
01:09:45.330 --> 01:09:48.570
He really needs to get behind this new way of doing things.

1710
01:09:49.390 --> 01:09:51.690
And Michael's like, no, you know what?

1711
01:09:51.710 --> 01:09:54.910
I'm going to prove you wrong that the old way is the best way.

1712
01:09:55.090 --> 01:09:55.930
I'm going to prove you wrong.

1713
01:09:56.050 --> 01:09:57.230
We don't know what that's going to be.

1714
01:09:57.370 --> 01:09:58.810
But Michael says that.

1715
01:09:59.110 --> 01:10:01.090
And Jenna, there is a deleted scene.

1716
01:10:01.090 --> 01:10:01.870
I have to tell you,

1717
01:10:02.030 --> 01:10:15.690
there's another department that's very stressed out about this because Ryan has a moment with the accountants where he shows them the new program they're going to use online for all of the accounting of Dunder Mifflin.

1718
01:10:16.110 --> 01:10:18.670
And it's basically going to make them obsolete.

1719
01:10:20.050 --> 01:10:20.310
Wow.

1720
01:10:22.050 --> 01:10:28.710
So people are freaking out about this, this new technology that Dunder Mifflin is going to be using.

1721
01:10:29.430 --> 01:10:34.530
Well, we're going to get more of Michael's reaction to all of this in the next episode.

1722
01:10:35.650 --> 01:10:41.470
We end this episode, though, with Angela and Dwight's dinner date.

1723
01:10:42.150 --> 01:10:42.670
Yes.

1724
01:10:43.010 --> 01:10:45.510
Well, lady, I have some location trivia.

1725
01:10:45.810 --> 01:10:47.950
I know you love a good location story.

1726
01:10:48.370 --> 01:10:49.270
I do.

1727
01:10:49.270 --> 01:10:57.150
And I will tell you, Ivy Burke will be very excited to hear what you have to say because Ivy would like to know where you filmed the Dwight and Angela dinner.

1728
01:10:57.150 --> 01:11:00.890
We filmed it in the most beautiful neighborhood.

1729
01:11:01.360 --> 01:11:03.830
It's called the Malibu Lake Mountain Club.

1730
01:11:04.430 --> 01:11:10.690
It's a private community, so much so that there are no signs off the freeway for it.

1731
01:11:11.350 --> 01:11:11.850
Wow.

1732
01:11:11.850 --> 01:11:13.710
Yeah, it's like they don't want to be found.

1733
01:11:13.930 --> 01:11:15.530
It's really, really beautiful.

1734
01:11:15.930 --> 01:11:18.550
This neighborhood was created in 1924.

1735
01:11:18.950 --> 01:11:20.790
It was just like a valley.

1736
01:11:21.330 --> 01:11:24.390
And they made a lake, a man-made lake.

1737
01:11:24.550 --> 01:11:27.230
And the members really wanted to keep it a secret.

1738
01:11:27.570 --> 01:11:32.630
So they removed the highway signs that gave directions to the lake, hoping to hide it from tourists.

1739
01:11:32.630 --> 01:11:38.170
In 1936, they built a clubhouse with a ballroom and a large patio.

1740
01:11:38.290 --> 01:11:43.550
And it had a speakeasy poker room and a mural of the original members.

1741
01:11:43.650 --> 01:11:47.530
And only members can use the lake and the swimming pool and the tennis court.

1742
01:11:47.690 --> 01:11:49.210
So it's this little private community.

1743
01:11:49.810 --> 01:11:52.850
Ronald Reagan was the honorary mayor.

1744
01:11:52.850 --> 01:11:56.750
And technically, that's the first political office he ever held.

1745
01:11:57.470 --> 01:11:57.810
Wow.

1746
01:11:58.250 --> 01:11:58.710
Mm-hmm.

1747
01:11:59.410 --> 01:12:01.330
Now, let me ask you a question.

1748
01:12:02.530 --> 01:12:09.590
Did you film at this location because this lake is going to feature prominently in the next episode?

1749
01:12:10.050 --> 01:12:10.950
Ding, ding, ding, ding, ding.

1750
01:12:11.190 --> 01:12:14.650
Okay, so we had this location for one day.

1751
01:12:15.130 --> 01:12:19.950
Dwight and Angela filmed their dinner date in the lodge at Malibu Lake, their restaurant.

1752
01:12:19.950 --> 01:12:25.610
And then we will see what happens to Michael and Dwight with the lake in the next episode.

1753
01:12:25.830 --> 01:12:27.610
But that was all the same day.

1754
01:12:27.730 --> 01:12:29.470
We filmed our scene at night.

1755
01:12:29.650 --> 01:12:31.530
So, Jenna, I had a late call time.

1756
01:12:31.730 --> 01:12:32.050
Oh.

1757
01:12:32.410 --> 01:12:34.090
There was this huge moon.

1758
01:12:34.090 --> 01:12:40.250
And after we did the scene, I walked out on this floating dock and just watched the moon for a minute.

1759
01:12:40.490 --> 01:12:43.890
And it was just like, you know, one of those magical nights on set where you're like,

1760
01:12:43.990 --> 01:12:47.410
this feels so cool because it's really a beautiful little area.

1761
01:12:47.410 --> 01:12:51.070
And Steve Carell has made their official website.

1762
01:12:51.270 --> 01:12:51.970
Ready for this?

1763
01:12:52.370 --> 01:12:56.490
It says, just a few of the famous faces that have shot here.

1764
01:12:56.770 --> 01:12:59.110
Charlie Chaplin, W.C.

1765
01:12:59.250 --> 01:13:03.470
Fields, Diane Lane, Lloyd Bridges, Lucille Ball, Steve Carell,

1766
01:13:03.970 --> 01:13:05.710
Betty Davis, Henry Fonda.

1767
01:13:05.930 --> 01:13:06.790
It goes on and on.

1768
01:13:06.950 --> 01:13:08.570
But Steve Carell made the liSt.

1769
01:13:08.770 --> 01:13:10.050
I love that.

1770
01:13:10.390 --> 01:13:11.650
I know, right?

1771
01:13:11.790 --> 01:13:12.390
Really fun.

1772
01:13:12.390 --> 01:13:16.710
But this scene between Rain and I, I just loved doing it.

1773
01:13:17.030 --> 01:13:22.430
We took the scene apart because Dwight is hoping that it's one thing.

1774
01:13:23.090 --> 01:13:25.710
Angela's trying for it to be a reconciliation.

1775
01:13:26.270 --> 01:13:27.930
And then she just can't do it.

1776
01:13:28.490 --> 01:13:28.690
Yeah.

1777
01:13:28.850 --> 01:13:32.350
And they wanted me to be able to show that emotion in my face that I was trying.

1778
01:13:33.070 --> 01:13:34.750
And then I just couldn't get past it.

1779
01:13:34.890 --> 01:13:35.870
I couldn't get past it.

1780
01:13:35.870 --> 01:13:39.890
And it's got a couplet of dialogue that I love.

1781
01:13:40.310 --> 01:13:43.290
It's when Angela says, I heard a joke today.

1782
01:13:43.590 --> 01:13:46.410
And Dwight says, oh, that's funny.

1783
01:13:46.690 --> 01:13:49.690
And Angela says, yes, it was.

1784
01:13:52.090 --> 01:13:52.250
Yeah.

1785
01:13:54.540 --> 01:13:57.140
Well, this scene is just beautiful.

1786
01:13:57.440 --> 01:14:00.000
It's beautiful for all the reasons you said.

1787
01:14:00.240 --> 01:14:05.820
It's funny, but then it breaks your heart because your performance, Angela,

1788
01:14:05.820 --> 01:14:10.140
we really see your character's heart breaking in this moment.

1789
01:14:10.380 --> 01:14:12.380
And it's just real.

1790
01:14:12.620 --> 01:14:14.520
Like, you aren't playing it for comedy.

1791
01:14:14.520 --> 01:14:15.680
It's just real.

1792
01:14:16.240 --> 01:14:21.660
And these are the moments of The Office that I'm so proud of as a television comedy

1793
01:14:21.660 --> 01:14:24.740
that we took these moments to get really real.

1794
01:14:26.100 --> 01:14:26.700
Thanks, lady.

1795
01:14:26.700 --> 01:14:27.280
I loved it.

1796
01:14:27.460 --> 01:14:27.760
Thanks.

1797
01:14:27.860 --> 01:14:29.400
I really enjoyed the storyline.

1798
01:14:29.820 --> 01:14:32.760
I love Dwight and Angela being together.

1799
01:14:32.940 --> 01:14:35.540
And I loved when they were at odds.

1800
01:14:35.540 --> 01:14:37.380
It was equally fun to play.

1801
01:14:37.880 --> 01:14:38.360
Yeah.

1802
01:14:38.600 --> 01:14:39.840
It's such a good dynamic.

1803
01:14:40.200 --> 01:14:44.420
Well, you know, Jenna, one of the things that got me so tickled was that during this time,

1804
01:14:44.660 --> 01:14:51.500
the Dwight-Angelo relationship was compared by a TV journalist to Hot Lips Houlihan and Frank

1805
01:14:51.500 --> 01:14:52.400
from MASH.

1806
01:14:52.520 --> 01:14:55.360
And that was a show I grew up watching with my dad.

1807
01:14:55.360 --> 01:14:57.360
And I just was so tickled by that.

1808
01:14:57.720 --> 01:14:58.740
Oh, that's perfect.

1809
01:14:59.120 --> 01:15:03.300
And that was Dunder Mifflin Infinity Part 1.

1810
01:15:03.620 --> 01:15:04.180
Yes.

1811
01:15:04.180 --> 01:15:09.380
And I would like to say thank you to Joya Balfour for giving us the digital media inside

1812
01:15:09.380 --> 01:15:09.980
info.

1813
01:15:10.100 --> 01:15:15.620
And you can check out her website at joyabalfour.com for some fun stuff that they were doing at

1814
01:15:15.620 --> 01:15:16.520
NBC.com.

1815
01:15:17.340 --> 01:15:23.140
Well, when we pick up this storyline, we're going to have some lovely gift baskets and a

1816
01:15:23.140 --> 01:15:24.140
huge stunt.

1817
01:15:24.540 --> 01:15:26.020
Especially for our show.

1818
01:15:26.220 --> 01:15:27.480
I was so impressed.

1819
01:15:27.480 --> 01:15:29.440
I can't wait to talk about it.

1820
01:15:29.600 --> 01:15:31.340
